1.Problem Statement

A. Modify the program to accept additional vertices or edges and display the updated graph or result.
B. Modify the program to compare the relevant traversal, MST, or shortest-path results before and after an update.
C. Modify the program to handle disconnected vertices or an unavailable path and display an appropriate message\.

2. Approach
1. Initialize an empty graph using an adjacency matrix to represent connections between vertices.
2. Allow the user to create a directed or undirected graph by entering vertices.
3. Provide options to insert additional vertices and edges dynamically.
4. Display the updated graph using an adjacency matrix and adjacency list.
5. Implement Breadth-First Search (BFS) using a queue and Depth-First Search (DFS) using a stack.
6. Save the graph's state before each successful vertex or edge insertion to compare BFS results before and after the latest update.
7. Identify newly reachable vertices and report vertices that cannot be reached from the selected starting vertex.
8. Check whether a path exists between two selected vertices and display the shortest path in terms of the number of edges using BFS.
9. Display appropriate error messages for invalid vertices, duplicate vertices, existing edges, and unavailable paths.
10. Repeat the menu until the user selects the Exit option.

3. Conclusion
The modified menu-driven Graph Toolkit was successfully implemented in C++. 
It supports dynamic insertion of vertices and edges, graph representation using adjacency matrix and adjacency list, 
BFS and DFS traversals, comparison of traversal results before and after updates, and shortest-path detection. 
It also handles disconnected vertices and unavailable paths with appropriate messages. 
The modifications improve the program's flexibility and demonstrate practical applications of graph data 
structures and traversal algorithms.
